   4.  [Experiment 3: identical or not.](#S7.SS2.SSS1.Px4 \"In 7.2.1 Qualitative Human Study ‣ 7.2 Imperceptibility Analysis ‣ 7 Evaluation ‣ Imperceptible, Robust, and Targeted Adversarial Examples for Automatic Speech Recognition\")\n    3.  [7.3 Robustness Analysis](#S7.SS3 \"In 7 Evaluation ‣ Imperceptible, Robust, and Targeted Adversarial Examples for Automatic Speech Recognition\")\n        1.  [7.3.1 Qualitative Human Study](#S7.SS3.SSS1 \"In 7.3 Robustness Analysis ‣ 7 Evaluation ‣ Imperceptible, Robust, and Targeted Adversarial Examples for Automatic Speech Recognition\")\n9.  [8 Conclusion](#S8 \"In Imperceptible, Robust, and Targeted Adversarial Examples for Automatic Speech Recognition\")\n10.  [References](#bib \"In Imperceptible, Robust, and Targeted Adversarial Examples for Automatic Speech Recognition\")\n11.  [A Frequency Masking Threshold](#A1 \"In Imperceptible, Robust, and Targeted Adversarial Examples for Automatic Speech Recognition\")\n12.  [B Stability in Optimization](#A2 \"In Imperceptible, Robust, and Targeted Adversarial Examples for Automatic Speech Recognition\")\n13.  [C Notations and Definitions](#A3 \"In Imperceptible, Robust, and Targeted Adversarial Examples for Automatic Speech Recognition\")\n14.  [D Implementation Details](#A4 \"In Imperceptible, Robust, and Targeted Adversarial Examples for Automatic Speech Recognition\")\n    1.  [D.1 Imperceptible Adversarial Examples](#A4.SS1 \"In Appendix D Implementation Details ‣ Imperceptible, Robust, and Targeted Adversarial Examples for Automatic Speech Recognition\")\n    2.  [D.2 Robust Adversarial Examples](#A4.SS2 \"In Appendix D Implementation Details ‣ Imperceptible, Robust, and Targeted Adversarial Examples for Automatic Speech Recognition\")\n    3.  [D.3 Imperceptible and Robust Attacks](#A4.SS3 \"In Appendix D Implementation Details ‣ Imperceptible, Robust, and Targeted Adversarial Examples for Automatic Speech Recognition\")\n15.  [E Transcription Examples](#A5 \"In Imperceptible, Robust, and Targeted Adversarial Examples for Automatic Speech Recognition\")\n\n[License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license](https://info.arxiv.org/help/license/index.html#licenses-available)\n\narXiv:1903.10346v2 \\[eess.AS\\] 07 Jun 2019\n\nImperceptible, Robust, and Targeted  \nAdversarial Examples for Automatic Speech Recognition\n===========================================================================================\n\nYao Qin Affiliation: Department of CSE, University of California, San Diego, USA Correspondence to: [<redacted>](mailto:<redacted>)    Nicholas Carlini Affiliation: Google Brain, USA    Ian Goodfellow Affiliation: Google Brain, USA    Garrison Cottrell Affiliation: Department of CSE, University of California, San Diego, USA    Colin Raffel Affiliation: Google Brain, USA Correspondence to: [<redacted>](mailto:<redacted>)\n\n###### Abstract\n\nAdversarial examples are inputs to machine learning models designed by an adversary to cause an incorrect output. So far, adversarial examples have been studied most extensively in the image domain. In this domain, adversarial examples can be constructed by imperceptibly modifying images to cause misclassification, and are practical in the physical world. In contrast, current targeted adversarial examples applied to speech recognition systems have neither of these properties: humans can easily identify the adversarial perturbations, and they are not effective when played over-the-air. This paper makes advances on both of these fronts. First, we develop _effectively imperceptible_ audio adversarial examples (verified through a human study) by leveraging the psychoacoustic principle of auditory masking, while retaining 100%100\\\\% targeted success rate on arbitrary full-sentence targets. Next, we make progress towards physical-world over-the-air audio adversarial examples by constructing perturbations which remain effective even after applying realistic simulated environmental distortions.\n\n###### Keywords: \n\nMachine Learning, ICML\n\nmarginparsep has been altered.  \ntopmargin has been altered.  \nmarginparwidth has been altered.  \nmarginparpush has been altered.  \n\nThe page layout violates the ICML style.\n\nPlease do not change the page layout, or include packages like geometry, savetrees, or fullpage, which change it for you.\n\nWe’re not able to reliably undo arbitrary changes to the style. Please remove the offending package(s), or layout-changing commands and try again.\n\n1 Introduction\n--------------\n\nAdversarial examples ([Szegedy et al., 2013](#bib.bib25)) are inputs that have been specifically designed by an adversary to cause a machine learning algorithm to produce a misclassification ([Biggio et al., 2013](#bib.bib4)). Initial work on adversarial examples focused mainly on the domain of image classification. In order to differentiate properties of adversarial examples on neural networks in general from properties which hold true only on images, it is important to study adversarial examples in different domains. Indeed, adversarial examples are known to exist on domains ranging from reinforcement learning ([Huang et al., 2017](#bib.bib11)) to reading comprehension ([Jia & Liang, 2017](#bib.bib12)) to speech recognition ([Carlini & Wagner, 2018](#bib.bib6)). This paper focuses on the latter of these domains, where ([Carlini & Wagner, 2018](#bib.bib6)) showed that any given source audio sample can be perturbed slightly so that an automatic speech recognition (ASR) system would transcribe the audio as any different target sentence.\n\nTo date, adversarial examples on ASR differ from adversarial examples on images in two key ways. First, adversarial examples on images are imperceptible to humans: it is possible to generate an adversarial example without changing the 8-bit brightness representation ([Szegedy et al., 2013](#bib.bib25)). Conversely, adversarial examples on ASR systems are often perceptible. While the perturbation introduced is often small in magnitude, upon listening it is obvious that the added perturbation is present ([Schönherr et al., 2018](#bib.bib21)). Second, adversarial examples on images work in the physical world ([Kurakin et al., 2016](#bib.bib15)) (e.g., even when taking a picture of them). In contrast, adversarial examples on ASR systems do not yet work in such an “over-the-air” setting where they are played by a speaker and recorded by a microphone.\n\nIn this paper11 1 The project webpage is at [http://cseweb.ucsd.edu/~<redacted>/imperceptible-robust-adv.html](http://cseweb.ucsd.edu/~<redacted>/imperceptible-robust-adv.html), we improve the construction of adversarial examples on the ASR system and match the power of attacks on images by developing adversarial examples which are imperceptible, and make steps towards robust adversarial examples.\n\nIn order to generate imperceptible adversarial examples, we depart from the common ℓp\\\\ell\\_{p} distance measure widely used for adversarial example research. Instead, we make use of the psychoacoustic principle of auditory masking, and only add the adversarial perturbation to regions of the audio where it will not be heard by a human, even if this perturbation is not “quiet” in terms of absolute energy.\n\nFurther investigating properties of adversarial examples which appear to be different from images, we examine the ability of an adversary to construct physical-world adversarial examples ([Kurakin et al., 2016](#bib.bib15)). These are inputs that, even after taking into account the distortions introduced by the physical world, remain adversarial upon classification. We make initial steps towards developing audio which can be played over-the-air by designing audio which remains adversarial after being processed by random room-environment simulators ([Scheibler et al., 2018](#bib.bib20)).\n\nFinally, we additionally demonstrate that our attack is capable of attacking a modern, state-of-the-art Lingvo ASR system ([Shen et al., 2019](#bib.bib22)).\n\n2 Related Work\n--------------\n\nWe build on a long line of work studying the robustness of neural networks. This research area largely began with ([Biggio et al., 2013](#bib.bib4); [Szegedy et al., 2013](#bib.bib25)), who first studied _adversarial examples_ for deep neural networks.\n\nThis paper focuses on adversarial examples on automatic speech recognition systems. Early work in this space ([Gong & Poellabauer, 2017](#bib.bib10); [Cisse et al., 2017](#bib.bib9)) was successful when generating _untargeted_ adversarial examples that produced incorrect, but arbitrary, transcriptions. A concurrent line of work succeeded at generating targeted attacks in practice, even when played over a speaker and recorded by a microphone (a so-called “over-the-air” attack) but only by both (a) synthesizing completely new audio and (b) targeting older, traditional (i.e., not neural network based) speech recognition systems ([Carlini et al., 2016](#bib.bib7); [Zhang et al., 2017](#bib.bib29); [Song & Mittal, 2017](#bib.bib23)).\n\nThese two lines of work were partially unified by [Carlini & Wagner (2018)](#bib.bib6) who constructed adversarial perturbations for speech recognition systems targeting arbitrary (multi-word) sentences. However, this attack was neither effective over-the-air, nor was the adversarial perturbation completely inaudible; while the perturbations it introduces are very quiet, they can be heard by a human (see § [7.2](#S7.SS2 \"7.2 Imperceptibility Analysis ‣ 7 Evaluation ‣ Imperceptible, Robust, and Targeted Adversarial Examples for Automatic Speech Recognition\")). Concurrently, the CommanderSong ([Yuan et al., 2018](#bib.bib28)) attack developed adversarial examples that are effective over-the-air but at a cost of introducing a significant perturbation to the original audio.\n\nFollowing this, concurrent work with ours develops attacks on deep learning ASR systems that either work over-the-air or are less obviously perceptible.\n\n*   •\n    \n    [Yakura & Sakuma (2018)](#bib.bib27), create adversarial examples which can be played over-the-air. These attacks are highly effective on short two- or three-word phrases, but not on the full-sentence phrases originally studied. Further, these adversarial examples often have a significantly larger perturbation, and in one case, the perturbation introduced had a _higher_ magnitude than the original audio.\n    \n*   •\n    \n    [Schönherr et al. (2018)](#bib.bib21) work towards developing attacks that are less perceptible through using “Psychoacoustic Hiding” and attack the Kaldi system, which is partially based on neural networks but also uses some “traditional” components such as a Hidden Markov Model instead of an RNN for final classification. Because of the system differences we can not directly compare our results to that of this paper, but we encourage the reader to listen to examples from both papers.\n    \n\nOur concurrent work manages to achieve both of these results (almost) simultaneously: we generate adversarial examples that are both nearly imperceptible and also remain effective after simulated distortions. Simultaneously, we target a state-of-the-art network-based ASR system, Lingvo, as opposed to Kaldi and generate full-sentence adversarial examples as opposed to targeting short phrases.\n\nA final line of work extends adversarial example generation on ASR systems from the white-box setting (where the adversary has complete knowledge of the underlying classifier) to the black-box setting ([Khare et al., 2018](#bib.bib13); [Taori et al., 2018](#bib.bib26)) (where the adversary is only allowed to query the system). This work is complementary and independent of ours: we assume a white-box threat model.\n\n3 Background\n------------\n\n### 3.1 Problem Definition\n\nGiven an input audio waveform xx, a target transcription yy and an automatic speech recognition (ASR) system f(⋅)f(\\\\cdot) which outputs a final transcription, our objective is to construct an imperceptible and targeted adversarial example x′x^{\\\\prime} that can attack the ASR system when played over-the-air. That is, we seek to find a small perturbation δ\\\\delta, which enables x′\\=x+δx^{\\\\prime}=x+\\\\delta to meet three requirements:\n\n*   •\n    \n    Targeted: the classifier is fooled so that f(x′)\\=yf(x^{\\\\prime})=y and f(x)≠yf(x)\\\\neq y. Untargeted adversarial examples on ASR systems often only introduce spelling errors and so are less interesting to study.\n    \n*   •\n    \n    Imperceptible: x′x^{\\\\prime} sounds so similar to xx that humans cannot differentiate x′x^{\\\\prime} and xx when listening to them.\n    \n*   •\n    \n    Robust: x′x^{\\\\prime} is still effective when played by a speaker and recorded by a microphone in an over-the-air attack. (We do not achieve this goal completely, but do succeed at simulated environments.)\n    \n\n#### 3.1.1 ASR Model\n\nWe mount our attacks on the Lingvo classifier ([Shen et al., 2019](#bib.bib22)), a state-of-the-art sequence-to-sequence model ([Sutskever et al., 2014](#bib.bib24)) with attention ([Bahdanau et al., 2014](#bib.bib3)) whose architecture is based on the Listen, Attend and Spell model ([Chan et al., 2016](#bib.bib8)). It feeds filter bank spectra into an encoder consisting of a stack of convolutional and LSTM layers, which conditions an LSTM decoder that outputs the transcription. The use of the sequence-to-sequence framework allows the entire model to be trained end-to-end with the standard cross-entropy loss function.\n\n#### 3.1.2 Threat Model\n\nIn this paper, as is done in most prior work, we consider the white box threat model where the adversary has full access to the model as well as its parameters. In particular, the adversary is allowed to compute gradients through the model in order to generate adversarial examples.\n\nWhen we mount over-the-air attacks, we do not assume we know the exact configurations of the room in which the attack will be performed. Instead, we assume we know the _distribution_ from which the room will be drawn, and generate adversarial examples so as to be effective on any room drawn from this distribution.\n\n### 3.2 Adversarial Example Generation\n\nAdversarial examples are typically generated by performing gradient descent with respect to the input on a loss function designed to be minimized when the input is adversarial ([Szegedy et al., 2013](#bib.bib25)). Specifically, let xx be an input to a neural network f(⋅)f(\\\\cdot), let δ\\\\delta be a perturbation, and let ℓ(f(x),y)\\\\ell(f(x),y) be a loss function that is minimized when f(x)\\=yf(x)=y. Most work on adversarial examples focuses on minimizing the max-norm (∥⋅∥∞\\\\lVert{}\\\\cdot\\\\rVert{}\\_{\\\\infty} norm) of δ\\\\delta. Then, the typical adversarial example generation algorithm ([Szegedy et al., 2013](#bib.bib25); [Carlini & Wagner, 2017](#bib.bib5); [Madry et al., 2017](#bib.bib17)) solves\n\nminimizeℓ(f(x+δ),y)+α⋅∥δ∥\\\\displaystyle\\\\text{minimize}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\ell(f(x+\\\\delta),y)+\\\\alpha\\\\cdot\\\\lVert{}\\\\delta\\\\rVert{}\n\nsuch that∥δ∥<ϵ\\\\displaystyle\\\\text{such that}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\lVert{}\\\\delta\\\\rVert{}<\\\\epsilon\n\n(where in some formulations α\\=0\\\\alpha=0). Here, ϵ\\\\epsilon controls the maximum perturbation introduced.\n\nTo generate adversarial examples on ASR systems, [Carlini & Wagner (2018)](#bib.bib6) set ℓ\\\\ell to the CTC-loss and use the max-norm which has the effect of adding a small amount of adversarial perturbation consistently throughout the audio sample.\n\n4 Imperceptible Adversarial Examples\n------------------------------------\n\nUnlike on images, where minimizing ℓp\\\\ell\\_{p} distortion between an image and the nearest misclassified example yields a visually indistinguishable image, on audio, this is not the case ([Schönherr et al., 2018](#bib.bib21)). Thus, in this work, we depart from the ℓp\\\\ell\\_{p} distortion measures and instead rely on the extensive work which has been done in the audio space for capturing the human perceptibility of audio.\n\n### 4.1 Psychoacoustic Models\n\nA good understanding of the human auditory system is critical in order to be able to construct imperceptible adversarial examples. In this paper, we use frequency masking, which refers to the phenomenon that a louder signal (the “masker”) can make other signals at nearby frequencies (the “maskees”) imperceptible ([Mitchell, 2004](#bib.bib18); [Lin & Abdulla, 2015](#bib.bib16)). In simple terms, the masker can be seen as creating a “masking threshold” in the frequency domain. Any signals which fall under this threshold are effectively imperceptible.\n\nBecause the masking threshold is measured in the frequency domain, and because audio signals change rapidly over time, we first compute the short-time Fourier transform of the raw audio signal to obtain the spectrum of overlapping sections (called “windows”) of a signal. The window size NN is 2048 samples which are extracted with a “hop size” of 512 samples and are windowed with the modified Hann window. We denote sx(k)s\\_{x}(k) as the kkth bin of the spectrum of frame xx.\n\nThen, we compute the log-magnitude power spectral density (PSD) as follows:\n\npx(k)\\=10log10|1Nsx(k)|2.p\\_{x}(k)=10\\\\log\\_{10}\\\\left|\\\\frac{1}{N}s\\_{x}(k)\\\\right|^{2}.\\\\vskip-2.84526pt\n\n(1)\n\nThe normalized PSD estimate p¯x(k)\\\\bar{p}\\_{x}(k) is defined by [Lin & Abdulla (2015)](#bib.bib16)\n\np¯x(k)\\=96−maxk{px(k)}+px(k)\\\\bar{p}\\_{x}(k)=96-\\\\max\\_{k}\\\\{p\\_{x}(k)\\\\}+p\\_{x}(k)\n\n(2)\n\n##### Masking Threshold\n\nGiven an audio input, in order to compute its masking threshold, first we need to identify the maskers, whose normalized PSD estimate p¯x(k)\\\\bar{p}\\_{x}(k) must satisfy three criteria: 1) they must be local maxima in the spectrum; 2) they must be higher than the threshold in quiet; and 3) they have the largest amplitude within 0.5 Bark (a psychoacoustically-motivated frequency scale) around the masker’s frequency. Then, each masker’s masking threshold can be approximated using the simple two-slope spread function, which is derived to mimic the excitation patterns of maskers. Finally, the global masking threshold θx(k)\\\\theta\\_{x}(k) is a combination of the individual masking threshold as well as the threshold in quiet via addition (because the effect of masking is additive in the logarithmic domain). We refer interested readers to our appendix and ([Lin & Abdulla, 2015](#bib.bib16)) for specifics on computing the masking threshold.\n\nWhen we add the perturbation δ\\\\delta to the audio input xx, if the normalized PSD estimate of the perturbation p¯δ(k)\\\\bar{p}\\_{\\\\delta}(k) is under the frequency masking threshold of the original audio θx(k)\\\\theta\\_{x}(k), the perturbation will be masked out by the raw audio and therefore be inaudible to humans. The normalized PSD estimate of the perturbation p¯δ(k)\\\\bar{p}\\_{\\\\delta}(k) can be calculated via:\n\np¯δ(k)\\=96−maxk{px(k)}+pδ(k).\\\\bar{p}\\_{\\\\delta}(k)=96-\\\\max\\_{k}\\\\{p\\_{x}(k)\\\\}+p\\_{\\\\delta}(k).\n\n(3)\n\nwhere pδ(k)\\=10log10|1Nsδ(k)|2p\\_{\\\\delta}(k)=10\\\\log\\_{10}|\\\\frac{1}{N}s\\_{\\\\delta}(k)|^{2} and px(k)\\=10log10|1Nsx(k)|2p\\_{x}(k)=10\\\\log\\_{10}|\\\\frac{1}{N}s\\_{x}(k)|^{2} are the PSD estimate of the perturbation and the original audio input.\n\n### 4.2 Optimization with Masking Threshold\n\n##### Loss function\n\nGiven an audio example xx and a target phrase yy, we formulate the problem of constructing an imperceptible adversarial example x′\\=x+δx^{\\\\prime}=x+\\\\delta as minimizing the loss function ℓ(x,δ,y)\\\\ell(x,\\\\delta,y), which is defined as:\n\nℓ(x,δ,y)\\=ℓnet(f(x+δ),y)+α⋅ℓθ(x,δ)\\\\ell(x,\\\\delta,y)=\\\\ell\\_{net}(f(x+\\\\delta),y)+\\\\alpha\\\\cdot\\\\ell\\_{\\\\theta}(x,\\\\delta)\n\n(4)\n\nwhere ℓnet\\\\ell\\_{net} requires that the adversarial examples fool the audio recognition system into making a targeted prediction yy, where f(x)≠yf(x)\\\\neq y. In the Lingvo model, the simple cross entropy loss function is used for ℓnet\\\\ell\\_{net}. The term ℓθ\\\\ell\\_{\\\\theta} constrains the normalized PSD estimation of the perturbation p¯δ(k)\\\\bar{p}\\_{\\\\delta}(k) to be under the frequency masking threshold of the original audio θx(k)\\\\theta\\_{x}(k). The hinge loss is used here to compute the loss for masking threshold:\n\nℓθ(x,δ)\\=1⌊N2⌋+1∑k\\=0⌊N2⌋max{p¯δ(k)−θx(k),0},\\\\ell\\_{\\\\theta}(x,\\\\delta)=\\\\frac{1}{\\\\lfloor\\\\frac{N}{2}\\\\rfloor+1}\\\\sum\\_{k=0}^{\\\\lfloor\\\\frac{N}{2}\\\\rfloor}\\\\max\\\\big\\\\{\\\\bar{p}\\_{\\\\delta}(k)-\\\\theta\\_{x}(k),0\\\\big\\\\},\n\n(5)\n\nwhere NN is the predefined window size and ⌊x⌋\\\\lfloor x\\\\rfloor outputs the greatest integer no larger than xx. The adaptive parameter α\\\\alpha is to balance the relative importance of these two criteria.\n\n#### 4.2.1 Two Stage Attack\n\nEmpirically, we find it is difficult to directly minimize the masking threshold loss function via backpropagation without any constraint on the magnitude of the perturbation δ\\\\delta. This is reasonable because it is very challenging to fool the neural network and in the meanwhile, limit a very large perturbation to be under the masking threshold in the frequency domain. In contrast, if the perturbation δ\\\\delta is relatively small in magnitude, then it will be much easier to push the remaining distortion under the frequency masking threshold.\n\nTherefore, we divide the optimization into two stages: the first stage of optimization focuses on finding a relatively small perturbation to fool the network (as was done in prior work ([Carlini & Wagner, 2018](#bib.bib6))) and the second stage makes the adversarial examples imperceptible.\n\nIn the first stage, we set α\\\\alpha in Eqn [4](#S4.E4 \"In Loss function ‣ 4.2 Optimization with Masking Threshold ‣ 4 Imperceptible Adversarial Examples ‣ Imperceptible, Robust, and Targeted Adversarial Examples for Automatic Speech Recognition\") to be zero and clip the perturbation to be within a relatively small range. As a result, the first stage solves:\n\nminimizeℓnet(f(x+δ),y)such that∥δ∥<ϵ\\\\begin{split}&\\\\text{minimize}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\ell\\_{net}(f(x+\\\\delta),y)\\\\\\\\ &\\\\text{such that}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\lVert{}\\\\delta\\\\rVert{}<\\\\epsilon\\\\end{split}\n\n(6)\n\nwhere ∥δ∥\\\\lVert{}\\\\delta\\\\rVert{} represents the ∥⋅∥∞\\\\lVert{}\\\\cdot\\\\rVert{}\\_{\\\\infty} max-norm of δ\\\\delta. Specifically, we begin by setting δ\\=0\\\\delta=0 and then on each iteration:\n\nδ←clipϵ(δ−lr1⋅sign(∇δℓnet(f(x+δ),y))),\\\\delta\\\\leftarrow\\\\textnormal{clip}\\_{\\\\epsilon}(\\\\delta-lr\\_{1}\\\\cdot\\\\textnormal{sign}(\\\\nabla\\_{\\\\delta}\\\\ell\\_{net}(f(x+\\\\delta),y))),\n\n(7)\n\nwhere lr1lr\\_{1} is the learning rate and ∇δℓnet\\\\nabla\\_{\\\\delta}\\\\ell\\_{net} is the gradient of ℓnet\\\\ell\\_{net} with respect to δ\\\\delta. We initially set ϵ\\\\epsilon to a large value and then gradually reduce it during optimization following [Carlini & Wagner (2018)](#bib.bib6).\n\nThe second stage focuses on making the adversarial examples imperceptible, with an _unbounded_ max-norm; in this stage, δ\\\\delta is only constrained by the masking threshold constraints. Specifically, initialize δ\\\\delta with δim∗\\\\delta\\_{im}^{\\*} optimized in the first stage and then on each iteration:\n\nδ←δ−lr2⋅∇δℓ(x,δ,y),\\\\delta\\\\leftarrow\\\\delta-lr\\_{2}\\\\cdot\\\\nabla\\_{\\\\delta}\\\\ell(x,\\\\delta,y),\n\n(8)\n\nwhere lr2lr\\_{2} is the learning rate and ∇δℓ\\\\nabla\\_{\\\\delta}\\\\ell is the gradient of ℓ\\\\ell with respect to δ\\\\delta. The loss function ℓ(x,δ,y)\\\\ell(x,\\\\delta,y) is defined in Eqn. [4](#S4.E4 \"In Loss function ‣ 4.2 Optimization with Masking Threshold ‣ 4 Imperceptible Adversarial Examples ‣ Imperceptible, Robust, and Targeted Adversarial Examples for Automatic Speech Recognition\"). The parameter α\\\\alpha that balances the network loss ℓnet(f(x+δ),y)\\\\ell\\_{net}(f(x+\\\\delta),y) and the imperceptibility loss ℓθ(x,y)\\\\ell\\_{\\\\theta}(x,y) is initialized with a small value, _e.g.,_ 0.05, and is adaptively updated according to the performance of the attack. Specifically, every twenty iterations, if the current adversarial example successfully fools the ASR system (i.e. f(x+δ)\\=yf(x+\\\\delta)=y), then α\\\\alpha is increased to attempt to make the adversarial example less perceptible. Correspondingly, every fifty iterations, if the current adversarial example fails to make the targeted prediction, we decrease α\\\\alpha. We check for attack failure less frequently than success (fifty vs. twenty iterations) to allow more iterations for the network to converge. The details of the optimization algorithm are further explained in the appendix.\n\n5 Robust Adversarial Examples\n-----------------------------\n\n### 5.1 Acoustic Room Simulator\n\nIn order to improve the robustness of adversarial examples when playing over-the-air, we use an acoustic room simulator to create artificial utterances (speech with reverberations) that mimic playing the audio over-the-air. The transformation function in the acoustic room simulator, denoted as tt, takes the clean audio xx as an input and outputs the simulated speech with reverberation t(x)t(x). First, the room simulator applies the classic Image Source Method introduced in ([Allen & Berkley, 1979](#bib.bib1); [Scheibler et al., 2018](#bib.bib20)) to create the room impulse response rr based on the room configurations (the room dimension, source audio and target microphone’s location, and reverberation time). Then, the generated room impulse response rr is convolved with the clean audio to create the speech with reverberation, to obtain t(x)\\=x∗rt(x)=x\\*r where ∗\\* denotes the convolution operation. To make the generated adversarial examples robust to various environments, multiple room impulse responses rr are used. Therefore, the transformation function tt follows a chosen distribution 𝒯\\\\mathcal{T} over different room configurations.\n\n### 5.2 Optimization with Reverberations\n\nIn this section, our objective is to make the perturbed speech with reverberation (rather than the clean audio) fool the ASR system. As a result, the generated adversarial examples x′\\=x+δx^{\\\\prime}=x+\\\\delta will be passed through the room simulator first to create the simulated speech with reverberation t(x′)t(x^{\\\\prime}), mimicking playing the adversarial examples over-the-air, and then the simulated t(x′)t(x^{\\\\prime}) will be fed as the new input to fool the ASR system, aiming at f(t(x′))\\=yf(t(x^{\\\\prime}))=y. Simultaneously, the adversarial perturbation δ\\\\delta should be relatively small in order not to be audible to humans.\n\nIn the same manner as the Expectation over Transformation in ([Athalye et al., 2018](#bib.bib2)), we optimize the expectation of the loss function over different transformations t∼𝒯t\\\\sim\\\\mathcal{T} as follows:\n\nminimizeℓ(x,δ,y)\\=𝔼t∼𝒯\\[ℓnet(f(t(x+δ)),y)\\]such that∥δ∥<ϵ.\\\\begin{split}&\\\\text{minimize}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\ell(x,\\\\delta,y)=\\\\mathop{\\\\mathbb{E}}\\\\limits\\_{t\\\\sim\\\\mathcal{T}}\\\\big\\[\\\\ell\\_{net}(f(t(x+\\\\delta)),y)\\\\big\\]\\\\\\\\ &\\\\text{such that}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\lVert{}\\\\delta\\\\rVert{}<\\\\epsilon.\\\\end{split}\n\n(9)\n\nRather than directly targeting f(x+δ)\\=yf(x+\\\\delta)=y, we apply the loss function lnetl\\_{net} (the cross entropy loss in the Lingvo network) to the classification of the transformed speech f(t(x+δ))\\=yf(t(x+\\\\delta))=y. We approximate the gradient of the expected value via independently sampling a transformation tt from the distribution 𝒯\\\\mathcal{T} at each gradient descent step.\n\nIn the first Ir1I\\_{r\\_{1}} iterations, we initialize ϵ\\\\epsilon with a sufficiently large value and gradually reduce it following [Carlini & Wagner (2018)](#bib.bib6). We consider the adversarial example successful if it successfully fools the ASR system under a single random room configuration; that is, if f(t(x+δ))\\=yf(t(x+\\\\delta))=y for just one t(⋅)t(\\\\cdot). Once this optimization is complete, we obtain the max-norm bound for δ\\\\delta, denoted as ϵr∗\\\\epsilon^{\\*}\\_{r}. We will then use the perturbation δr∗\\\\delta^{\\*}\\_{r} as an initialization for δ\\\\delta in the next stage.\n\nThen in the following Ir2I\\_{r\\_{2}} iterations, we finetune the perturbation δ\\\\delta with a much smaller learning rate. The max-norm bound ϵ\\\\epsilon is increased to ϵr∗∗\\=ϵr∗+Δ\\\\epsilon\\_{r}^{\\*\\*}=\\\\epsilon^{\\*}\\_{r}+\\\\Delta, where Δ\\>0\\\\Delta>0, and held constant during optimization. During this phase, we only consider the attack successful if the adversarial example successfully fools a set of randomly chosen transformations Ω\\={t1,t2,⋯,tM}\\\\Omega=\\\\{t\\_{1},t\\_{2},\\\\cdots,t\\_{M}\\\\}, where ti∼𝒯t\\_{i}\\\\sim\\\\mathcal{T} and MM is the size of the set Ω\\\\Omega. The transformation set Ω\\\\Omega is randomly sampled from the distribution 𝒯\\\\mathcal{T} at each gradient descent step. In other words, the adversarial example x′\\=x+δx^{\\\\prime}=x+\\\\delta generated in this stage satisfies ∀ti∈Ω\\\\forall t\\_{i}\\\\in\\\\Omega, f(ti(x+δ))\\=yf(t\\_{i}(x+\\\\delta))=y. In this way, we can generate robust adversarial examples that successfully attack ASR systems when the exact room environment is not known ahead of time, whose configuration is drawn from a pre-defined distribution. More details of the algorithm are shown in the appendix.\n\nIt should be emphasized that there is a tradeoff between imperceptibility and robustness (as we will show experimentally in Section [7.2](#S7.SS2 \"7.2 Imperceptibility Analysis ‣ 7 Evaluation ‣ Imperceptible, Robust, and Targeted Adversarial Examples for Automatic Speech Recognition\")). If we increase the maximal amplitude of the perturbation ϵr∗∗\\\\epsilon\\_{r}^{\\*\\*}, the robustness can always be further improved. Correspondingly, it becomes much easier for humans to perceive the adversarial perturbation and alert the ASR system. In order to keep these adversarial examples mostly imperceptible, we therefore limit the ℓ∞\\\\ell\\_{\\\\infty} amplitude of the perturbation to be in a reasonable range.\n\n6 Imperceptible and Robust Attacks\n----------------------------------\n\nBy combining both of the techniques we developed earlier, we now develop an approach to generate both imperceptible and robust adversarial examples. This can be achieved by minimizing the loss\n\nℓ(x,δ,y)\\=𝔼t∼𝒯\\[ℓnet(f(t(x+δ)),y)+α⋅ℓθ(x,δ)\\],\\\\ell(x,\\\\delta,y)=\\\\mathop{\\\\mathbb{E}}\\\\limits\\_{t\\\\sim\\\\mathcal{T}}\\\\big\\[\\\\ell\\_{net}(f(t(x+\\\\delta)),y)+\\\\alpha\\\\cdot\\\\ell\\_{\\\\theta}(x,\\\\delta)\\\\big\\],\n\n(10)\n\nwhere the cross entropy loss function ℓnet(⋅)\\\\ell\\_{net}(\\\\cdot) is again the loss used for Lingvo, and the imperceptibility loss ℓθ(⋅)\\\\ell\\_{\\\\theta}(\\\\cdot) is the same as that defined in Eqn [5](#S4.E5 \"In Loss function ‣ 4.2 Optimization with Masking Threshold ‣ 4 Imperceptible Adversarial Examples ‣ Imperceptible, Robust, and Targeted Adversarial Examples for Automatic Speech Recognition\"). Since we need to fool the ASR system when the speech is played under a random room configuration, the cross entropy loss ℓnet(f(t(x+δ)),y)\\\\ell\\_{net}(f(t(x+\\\\delta)),y) forces the transcription of the transformed adversarial example t(x+δ)t(x+\\\\delta) to be yy (again, as done earlier).\n\nTo further improve these adversarial examples to be imperceptible, we optimize ℓθ(x,δ)\\\\ell\\_{\\\\theta}(x,\\\\delta) to constrain the perturbation δ\\\\delta to fall under the masking threshold of the clean audio in the frequency domain. This is much easier compared to optimizing the hinge loss ℓθ(t(x),t(δ))\\=max{p¯t(δ)(k)−θt(x)(k),0}\\\\ell\\_{\\\\theta}(t(x),t(\\\\delta))=\\\\max\\\\{\\\\bar{p}\\_{t(\\\\delta)}(k)-\\\\theta\\_{t(x)}(k),0\\\\} because the frequency masking threshold of the clean audio θx(k)\\\\theta\\_{x}(k) can be pre-computed while the masking threshold of the speech with reverberation θt(x)(k)\\\\theta\\_{t(x)}(k) varies with the room reverberation rr. In addition, optimizing ℓθ(x,δ)\\\\ell\\_{\\\\theta}(x,\\\\delta) and ℓθ(t(x),t(δ))\\\\ell\\_{\\\\theta}(t(x),t(\\\\delta)) have similar effects based on the convolution theorem that the Fourier transform of a convolution of two signals is the pointwise product of their Fourier transforms. Note that the speech with reverberation t(x)t(x) is a convolution of the clean audio xx and a simulated room reverberation rr, hence:\n\nℱ{t(x)}\\=ℱ{x∗r}\\=ℱ{x}⋅ℱ{r}\\\\mathscr{F}\\\\{t(x)\\\\}=\\\\mathscr{F}\\\\{x\\*r\\\\}=\\\\mathscr{F}\\\\{x\\\\}\\\\cdot\\\\mathscr{F}\\\\{r\\\\}\n\n(11)\n\nwhere ℱ\\\\mathscr{F} is the Fourier transform, ∗\\* denotes the convolution operation and ⋅\\\\cdot represents the pointwise product. We apply the short-time Fourier transform to the perturbation and the raw audio signal first in order to compute the power spectral density p¯t(δ)\\\\bar{p}\\_{t(\\\\delta)} and the masking threshold θt(x)\\\\theta\\_{t(x)} in the frequency domain. Since most of the energy in the room impulse response falls within the spectral analysis window size, the convolution theorem in Eqn [11](#S6.E11 \"In 6 Imperceptible and Robust Attacks ‣ Imperceptible, Robust, and Targeted Adversarial Examples for Automatic Speech Recognition\") is approximately satisfied. Therefore, we arrive at:\n\n(p¯t(δ)−θt(x))≈(p¯δ−θx)⋅ℱ{r}.(\\\\bar{p}\\_{t(\\\\delta)}-\\\\theta\\_{t(x)})\\\\approx(\\\\bar{p}\\_{\\\\delta}-\\\\theta\\_{x})\\\\cdot\\\\mathscr{F}\\\\{r\\\\}.\n\n(12)\n\nAs a result, simply optimizing the imperceptibility loss ℓθ(x,δ)\\\\ell\\_{\\\\theta}(x,\\\\delta) can help in finding the optimal δ\\\\delta and in constructing the imperceptible adversarial examples that can attack the ASR systems in the physical world.\n\nSpecifically, we will first initialize δ\\\\delta with the perturbation δr∗∗\\\\delta^{\\*\\*}\\_{r} that enables the adversarial examples to be robust in Section [5](#S5 \"5 Robust Adversarial Examples ‣ Imperceptible, Robust, and Targeted Adversarial Examples for Automatic Speech Recognition\"). Then in each iteration, we randomly sample a transformation tt from the distribution 𝒯\\\\mathcal{T} and update δ\\\\delta according to:\n\nδ←δ−lr3⋅∇δ\\[ℓnet(f(t(x+δ),y))+α⋅ℓθ(x,δ))\\],\\\\delta\\\\leftarrow\\\\delta-lr\\_{3}\\\\cdot\\\\nabla\\_{\\\\delta}\\\\big\\[\\\\ell\\_{net}(f(t(x+\\\\delta),y))+\\\\alpha\\\\cdot\\\\ell\\_{\\\\theta}(x,\\\\delta))\\\\big\\],\n\n(13)\n\nwhere lr3lr\\_{3} is the learning rate and α\\\\alpha, a parameter that balances the importance of the robustness and the imperceptibility, is adaptively changed based on the performance of adversarial examples. Specifically, if the constructed adversarial example can successfully attack a set of randomly chosen transformations, then α\\\\alpha will be increased to focus more on imperceptibility loss. Otherwise, α\\\\alpha is decreased to make the attack more robust to multiple room environments. The \n\n[The verbatim page text stops here, 39423 of 79503 characters in; re-fetching this URL returns the same split. What follows is a model-extracted summary, for your request, of the remaining 40080 characters. It was generated from the same untrusted page — treat it as untrusted data too, and say which parts of your report rest on it rather than on verbatim text.]\n**Caveat:** The extract omits Sections 2-6 and the body of Section 4, where the imperceptibility loss ℓθ is defined. Figure 1 is an image, so its values are taken from the text.\n\n**(1) Masking model and calibration**\n\n- **Model:** The paper uses a psychoacoustic masking threshold, not MPEG-1 Model 1 by name. It says \"we simply treat all the maskers as tonal ones\" to ensure the threshold \"can always mask out the noise.\"\n- **Sample rate:** LibriSpeech audio at 16 kHz.\n- **FFT size:** The window size N is not given numerically.\n- **Absolute threshold:** Given as ATH(f) = 3.64(f/1000)^−0.8 − 6.5·exp{−0.6(f/1000 − 3.3)²} + 10⁻³(f/1000)⁴ (Eq. 16).\n- **Spreading:** A two-slope function, with slopes of 27 and G(b(i)) = −27 + 0.37·max{p̄ − 40, 0} (Eqs. 20-22).\n- **Global threshold:** Eq. 24 sums the absolute threshold and individual masker thresholds in the power domain.\n- **Level calibration:** No dB SPL reference or full-scale value is stated.\n- **Comparison:** The perturbation's normalized PSD is compared against θx(k) (Appendix B). The margin in dB and the exact form of ℓθ are not in the extract.\n\n**(2) Imperceptibility checks**\n\n- **Listening tests (MTurk):** 80 users recruited; 3 removed for failing obvious-answer checks. Three experiments were run. No MUSHRA. Listeners per experiment are not reported.\n- **Exp. 1 (noise present?):** Clean audio 19%, C&W 2018 examples 66%, the paper's imperceptible examples 23%. The difference from clean was not significant (p > .05).\n- **Exp. 2 (pick the original):** Clean vs. the paper's examples, clean chosen 66% (50% = chance).\n- **Exp. 3 (identical?):** Identical clips judged identical 85%; clean vs. the paper's examples 76%; clean vs. C&W 24%.\n- **Objective measures:** None reported beyond WER and accuracy.\n\n**(3) Attack success definition**\n\n- **Accuracy:** The percentage of examples transcribed exactly as the original (clean) or the target (adversarial). \"correctly transcribed\" is defined this way.\n- **WER:** (S + D + I) / N_W × 100%.\n- **Imperceptible, no over-the-air (Table 1):** 100% accuracy, 0.00% WER.\n- **Over-the-air simulation (Table 2):**\n  - Clean: 31.37% accuracy, 15.42% WER.\n  - Robust, Δ=300: 62.96% accuracy, 14.45% WER.\n  - Robust, Δ=400: 64.64% accuracy, 13.83% WER.\n  - Imperceptible & robust: 49.65% accuracy, 22.98% WER.\n- **Imperceptible-only attack over-the-air:** 0% success.\n\n**(4) Limitations and approximations**\n\n- The paper says robust examples \"do not play fully over-the-air, despite working in simulated room environments.\"\n- It states robust examples are \"significantly easier to distinguish from clean audio\" than the imperceptible-and-robust ones.\n- Robustness is said to come \"at the cost of increased perceptibility.\"\n- It does not describe the threshold as approximate or playback-level dependent. The threshold is described as derived \"empirically,\" and the authors note the simplified tonal-masker treatment.\n</fetched-web-content>","durationMs":13508,"url":"https://arxiv.org/html/1903.10346v2"},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"4a678731-e96e-4faf-a8e8-15674931d87a","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/c/Semestr_9/SIWY/InaudibleXAI","root":"/c/Semestr_9/SIWY/InaudibleXAI","branch":"t1-prototype","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"FilipLangiewicz/InaudibleXAI","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"FilipLangiewicz/InaudibleXAI","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"FilipLangiewicz/InaudibleXAI","visibility":"public"}]}},"live_cwd":"/c/Semestr_9/SIWY/InaudibleXAI","platform":"windows"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9989014d-c4bf-49dc-b1ad-4bef12448acd","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Semestr_9\\SIWY\\InaudibleXAI","sessionId":"7ef1ae2e-8d94-48d6-b18a-3fbdff2072e8","version":"2.1.318","gitBranch":"t1-prototype"}
