{"parentUuid":"cc7ccda1-2b5e-4900-a1ff-d62d4a3a2f64","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a052dc4a041b8a81b","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkZizXPDdzf9Nd8oFND1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AC8aw2VMtDBGR8UWFVJ1mq","name":"SubagentHandback","input":{"message":"Report on two psychoacoustic adversarial-ASR papers. Quotes are kept under 125 characters.\n\nACCESS AND SOURCE CAVEATS\n- The arXiv PDFs (https://arxiv.org/pdf/1808.05665 and https://arxiv.org/pdf/1903.10346) returned HTTP 200, but WebFetch got only compressed PDF streams with no readable text. The PDFs were also saved to a local file by the harness. Re-fetching them would return the same result.\n- I used the arXiv HTML (experimental) renderings linked from the abstract pages: https://arxiv.org/html/1808.05665v2 (Schönherr, arXiv v2, Oct 2018) and https://arxiv.org/html/1903.10346v2 (Qin, arXiv v2, Jun 2019).\n- Each HTML page was readable only for its first ~39k characters. The rest came back as a model-generated summary. Where I cite that summary I say so. Treat those items as unverified and check them against the original.\n- I did not access the NDSS 2019 proceedings version of Schönherr, or the ICML 2019 proceedings version of Qin. Numbers may differ between arXiv and camera-ready versions.\n- Section II-D (Schönherr's psychoacoustic model) and Sections 2-6 plus the body of Section 4 (Qin's loss and appendices) were not readable verbatim.\n\nPAPER A: Schönherr, Kohls, Zeiler, Holz, Kolossa, \"Adversarial Attacks Against ASR Systems via Psychoacoustic Hiding\" (NDSS 2019; arXiv 1808.05665)\n\n1. Masking model and threshold comparison\n- Model: MP3-style psychoacoustic model, not stated as MPEG-1 Model 1 in the readable text. Verbatim: \"We derive the respective set of thresholds for an audio input from the psychoacoustic model of MP3 compression.\"\n- Thresholds come from Zwicker et al. (verbatim, Introduction): \"hearing thresholds are calculated based on psychoacoustic experiments by Zwicker et al.\"\n- Framing (verbatim, II-D2): input is in buffers of \"1024 samples length that consist of two 576 sample granule windows.\" The FFT yields \"32 frequency bands\" split into \"MPEG ISO ... scale factor bands,\" measured in Bark.\n- Calibration (verbatim, II-D2): the matrices are \"normalized in such a way that the largest time-frequency-bin energy is limited to 95 dB.\" This is a normalization ceiling, not a dB SPL calibration. No dB SPL reference, playback level, or full-scale-to-SPL mapping is stated.\n- Sample rate, FFT size for the masking analysis, spreading function, and absolute threshold formula: not in the readable text.\n- Comparison (from the model summary, unverified): Φ = H − D, with D(t,k) = 20·log10(|S−M| / max|S|), then Φ* = Φ + λ with λ from 0 to 50 dB. Negative Φ* means the threshold is crossed, and those bins are zeroed. The margin is therefore in dB per time-frequency bin, relative to the utterance peak, not to SPL.\n- Verbatim on use: thresholds \"describe the amount of energy that can be added to the input in each individual window.\"\n\n2. Imperceptibility checks (verbatim from the Introduction, then the summary)\n- Verbatim: \"a two-part audibility study... The second part of the listening test is a MUSHRA test.\"\n- Transcription test (summary): 22 listeners. Mean WER 12.59% for original utterances vs 12.61% for adversarial ones. A two-sided t-test at 1% found no difference.\n- MUSHRA (summary): 30 listeners, 3 excluded, 9 stimuli (speech, music, birdsong). Samples with thresholds scored significantly above the no-threshold anchor at 1%.\n- Objective (summary): mean perceptibility ϕ of 10.11 dB with no thresholds and 5.49 dB at λ=20 (speech, 500 iterations). SNR 15.88 dB without thresholds vs 21.76 dB at λ=20, against 15.32 dB for CommanderSong.\n- Abstract (verbatim-ish): \"none of our target transcriptions were audible to human listeners.\"\n\n3. Attack success\n- Abstract: \"successful in up to 98% of cases... fewer than two minutes for a ten-second audio file\" (Kaldi).\n- Success definition (summary, quoted from the extract): \"An adversarial example was only counted as a success if it had a WER of 00 %.\" This is exact match with the target, unverified.\n- Table I reports mean WER (summary): 2.14% at 500 iterations with no thresholds, 36.43% at λ=20, above 100% at λ=0.\n- Backpropagation runs in blocks of 100 iterations, up to 5000 (summary).\n\n4. Approximation, playback level, artifacts\n- Verbatim (Intro): \"we apply a psychoacoustic model and manipulate the acoustic signal below the thresholds of human perception.\"\n- Verbatim (II-D1): \"we apply it as a rule set to add inaudible noise.\"\n- Summary: thresholds are \"tight,\" and λ is added \"to allow the algorithm to differ from the hearing thresholds by small amounts.\" The paper says \"almost imperceptible,\" not zero.\n- Summary: thresholds are \"calculated once and stored for the backpropagation.\"\n- Summary: MP3 encoding as a countermeasure also distorted the target transcription.\n- Playback level: not discussed in the readable text (summary).\n\nPAPER B: Qin, Carlini, Goodfellow, Cottrell, Raffel, \"Imperceptible, Robust, and Targeted Adversarial Examples for ASR\" (ICML 2019; arXiv 1903.10346)\n\n1. Masking model and threshold comparison (mostly verbatim, Section 4.1)\n- Model: frequency masking with a two-slope spread function. Verbatim: \"each masker's masking threshold can be approximated using the simple two-slope spread function.\"\n- Analysis: \"N is 2048 samples which are extracted with a 'hop size' of 512 samples and are windowed with the modified Hann window.\"\n- Sample rate: 16 kHz LibriSpeech (summary).\n- PSD (verbatim, Eq. 1): p_x(k) = 10 log10 |(1/N) s_x(k)|²\n- Normalization (verbatim, Eq. 2): p̄_x(k) = 96 − max_k{p_x(k)} + p_x(k)\n- The 96 is a normalization constant that sets the loudest bin to 96 on a relative scale. It is not a dB SPL calibration, and the paper does not say what 96 means physically.\n- Maskers (verbatim): they must be local maxima, above the threshold in quiet, and the largest within 0.5 Bark.\n- Absolute threshold (summary, Eq. 16): ATH(f) = 3.64(f/1000)^−0.8 − 6.5·exp{−0.6(f/1000 − 3.3)²} + 10⁻³(f/1000)⁴.\n- Spreading (summary): slopes of 27, and G(b(i)) = −27 + 0.37·max{p̄ − 40, 0}. The global threshold is summed in the power domain (summary, Eq. 24).\n- Comparison (verbatim): \"if the normalized PSD estimate of the perturbation p̄_δ(k) is under the frequency masking threshold θ_x(k)... the perturbation will be masked.\"\n- Hinge loss (verbatim, Eq. 5): ℓ_θ(x,δ) = (1/(⌊N/2⌋+1)) Σ max{p̄_δ(k) − θ_x(k), 0}. Margin is in normalized dB per bin.\n- The summary says all maskers are treated as tonal, so the threshold \"can always mask out the noise.\" This is unverified.\n\n2. Imperceptibility checks\n- Verbatim: \"effectively imperceptible audio adversarial examples (verified through a human study).\"\n- Study (summary): Amazon MTurk, 80 users recruited, 3 removed for failing obvious-answer checks. Three experiments. Listeners per experiment not reported. No MUSHRA.\n- Exp. 1, \"noise present?\" (summary): clean 19%, C&W 2018 examples 66%, this paper's imperceptible examples 23%. Difference from clean not significant (p > .05).\n- Exp. 2, pick the original (summary): clean chosen 66% against clean vs this paper's examples (50% is chance).\n- Exp. 3, identical? (summary): identical clips judged identical 85%; clean vs this paper's examples 76%; clean vs C&W 24%.\n- Objective measures: none beyond WER and accuracy (summary).\n\n3. Attack success\n- Targeted success, verbatim from the abstract: \"retaining 100% targeted success rate on arbitrary full-sentence targets.\"\n- Optimization success check (verbatim, 4.2.1): \"if the current adversarial example successfully fools the ASR system (i.e. f(x+δ)=y).\"\n- Robust-stage success (verbatim, 5.2): successful if \"f(t(x+δ))=y for just one t(·),\" and robust-stage check uses a random set Ω of transformations.\n- Accuracy/WER (summary): accuracy is the percentage transcribed exactly as the target. WER = (S+D+I)/N_W × 100%.\n- Table 1, imperceptible, no over-the-air (summary): 100% accuracy, 0.00% WER.\n- Table 2, over-the-air simulation (summary): clean 31.37% accuracy, 15.42% WER; robust Δ=300 62.96% / 14.45%; robust Δ=400 64.64% / 13.83%; imperceptible and robust 49.65% / 22.98%. Imperceptible-only attack over-the-air: 0% success.\n\n4. Approximation, playback level, artifacts\n- Verbatim: \"approximated using the simple two-slope spread function,\" and \"we simply treat all the maskers as tonal ones\" (summary, unverified quote).\n- Verbatim (Sec. 4): \"Any signals which fall under this threshold are effectively imperceptible.\"\n- Verbatim (Sec. 5.2): \"there is a tradeoff between imperceptibility and robustness.\" Increasing the perturbation bound \"it becomes much easier for humans to perceive the adversarial perturbation.\"\n- Verbatim (Sec. 3.1): robust examples do not achieve the robustness goal \"completely, but do succeed at simulated environments.\"\n- Playback level: not discussed in the readable text. No statement that the threshold is approximate beyond the spread-function approximation above.\n\nKEY DIFFERENCES FOR A REPLICATION\n- Neither paper gives a dB SPL calibration. Schönherr normalizes to a 95 dB ceiling on bin energy. Qin normalizes with 96 − max + p and is the only one with explicit equations.\n- Schönherr's threshold is MP3-derived and applied as a rule set, with λ slack. Qin's is an explicit hinge loss on normalized PSD, with a fixed 2048-sample STFT.\n- Both report results with listeners and transcription accuracy. Schönherr uses MUSHRA; Qin uses a three-experiment MTurk study with no MUSHRA."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":36385,"cache_read_input_to